#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161716 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161913 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3443d2b1-14c5-50ba-90b3-73b8bef47016" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Multiplication Problem Corresponding to a Model"}] :question/id #uuid "432754ac-d9f0-5843-a751-b13821e2e725" :question/math-academy-id "e-14520" :question/problem "![](images/01/0144d5fb5264162b7b2f03f71469f261c33c813ee235743c9e631edd7d4d049c.png)\n\nWhat multiplication problem is represented by the model above?" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{20}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/f0/f087c076a86b5bab709af23db87d63ed66363d4d580c23dc453573f264fb28bc.png)\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{5} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{6}{20}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161717 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161914 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d06ad10a-4be6-54c4-94b2-17a25f0c2c16" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Missing Number"}] :question/id #uuid "1fbd22af-5d6d-58ff-ac16-cfbb1d2577de" :question/math-academy-id "e-14523" :question/problem "![](images/23/23940bc011c6cd71c0d9d58b4bab0b175722a21fd1c80062ed5cb5ceff3729c0.png)\n\nWhat number is missing from the multiplication problem above?" :question/worked-solution "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{9}{20}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/31/31badccda3cc3548e2bdc40158e88d32feddcf92b8fdd4d4ae18f08396ca8cfc.png)\n\nThe shape on the right-hand side is split into $5$ equal parts by horizontal lines.\nSo, the shaded part on the right is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{5}\n$$\n\nof the shaded part on the left.\n\nTherefore, the multiplication problem shown is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{4} \\times \\dfrac{\\color{blue}3}{5} = \\dfrac{9}{20}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $\\color{blue}3$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161718 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161915 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "905c4fc9-5e62-5757-bee9-a9b277b62278" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Fraction Model Representing a Product of Two Fractions"}] :question/id #uuid "493da564-eed4-5ec7-b527-63fd3a68fc95" :question/math-academy-id "e-14528" :question/problem "What picture is missing from the multiplication model below?\n\n![](images/0d/0d381d096814ced0d6637ce373e14d77f317e1d8f202ed75371f744804d99f3c.png)" :question/worked-solution "In the model, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$\n\non the left.\n\nSince we need to multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}4}{\\color{blue}5}\n$$\n\n, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}5$ equal parts horizontally and ensure that $\\color{red}4$ of the parts remain shaded.\n\n![](images/97/978789f7aed1ea0e0dc8794aaeb00a3829168101e792129e72c0585fac43ebe2.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{8}{15}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore, the missing picture is as follows:\n\n![](images/fa/faf527aba9974aa23b9a5bffd8fc8f54a2135fce10bf2d09a7133556ab4b95e3.png)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161719 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161916 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa21548a-67bc-506c-8d6f-980aec5950dc" :knowledge-point/title "Multiplying Two Fractions Using Fraction Models"}] :question/id #uuid "2e14de76-f6e2-51f8-9590-8283fe81ce82" :question/math-academy-id "e-14539" :question/problem "![](images/60/6026c90a1101bf484ab649dcb35964ae4b136fc7784e384d1cf5d90ea47a96a2.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $\\dfrac{2}{6} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4}$." :question/worked-solution "Since we need to multiply by\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}3}{\\color{blue}4}\n$$\n\n, we split the whole into $\\color{blue}4$ equal parts horizontally and ensure that $\\color{red}3$ of the parts remain shaded.\n\n![](images/0f/0f7c5ff7155c00a811c744606ee039e764c6ded16f214a163b6d5d4a481e633b.png)\n\nFrom the picture on the right, we see that the shaded part represents\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{6}{24}\n$$\n\nof the whole.\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{2}{6} \\times \\dfrac{3}{4} = \\dfrac{6}{24}\n$$"}]}
